.page mkfifo 1 make named pipes (FIFOs)

# SYNOPSIS
*mkfifo* [*-m* _MODE_] _NAME_...

# DESCRIPTION
`mkfifo` creates a named pipe for each _NAME_. A named pipe is a file that two programs open to talk to each other: whatever one writes, the other reads, in order, without the data ever being stored. `open` for reading waits until another program has opened it for writing, and the other way round; a reader sees end of file when the last writer has closed it, and a writer without a reader gets `SIGPIPE`.

Named pipes can be made on the tmpfs file systems only: under `/tmp`, in the Linux root and the other RAM-backed directories. Anywhere else the call fails with "Operation not permitted".

# OPTIONS
@ *-m* _MODE_
    Give the new pipes the permissions _MODE_, an octal number. The default is 666 (changed by the umask).

# EXIT STATUS
0, 1 if a pipe could not be created or no name was given, 2 for an unknown option.

# EXAMPLES
    mkfifo /tmp/f
    cat /tmp/f &
    echo hello > /tmp/f
    mkfifo -m 600 /tmp/private

# SEE ALSO
mkdir(1), cat(1)
